rows=[
 ('logan-book',"Logan’s Run",'Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logan%27s_Run','secondary_reference','Original novel has a twenty-one-year death rule and a computer maintaining global infrastructure.','The food-processor and thirty-year material in this combined article describes the film; it is not novel evidence.'),
 ('logan-tv',"Logan’s Run television series",'Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logan%27s_Run_(TV_series)','secondary_reference','The television city is secretly governed by older human citizens, unlike the film, and has a thirty-year rule.','Distinct authority is established, but actual household production/access still requires TV-specific evidence; recycled footage is not a complete economic charter.'),
 ('baku-words','Star Trek: Insurrection — Sojef dialogue','IMDb contributors transcribing film dialogue','https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0120844/quotes?item=qt5534144','community_quote_of_primary','Sojef explicitly explains the choice not to use labor-saving machines in daily life.','Short dialogue transcription only, corroborated by the film summary; no unsupported egalitarian property or entitlement charter.'),
 ('baku-film','Star Trek: Insurrection','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Insurrection','secondary_reference','Technically advanced Ba’ku deliberately live simply; exile and later threatened displacement qualify the village scope.','The planet’s natural rejuvenation is not itself automated provision; the Son’a are a different population.'),
 ('gary','Gary Goodspeed','Final Space Wiki contributors','https://final-space.fandom.com/wiki/Gary_Goodspeed','secondary_lore','Gary serves five years repairing satellites aboard Galaxy One with machine companions.','Corrects the queued Infinity name to Galaxy One. Actual penal maintenance is work, and later lack of proper food is not the initial provision system.'),
 ('dwarf-machines','Vending Machine','Tongue Tied / Red Dwarf Wiki contributors','https://reddwarf.fandom.com/wiki/Vending_Machine','secondary_lore','TV dispensers prepare/deliver goods from cargo/stasis stores through automated tubes; access requires credits or Holly’s permission. Queeg temporarily blocks it.','This is long-term stored-input processing/service, not limitless matter creation; novel details and later series changes are not merged.'),
 ('dwarf-crew','The Crew','Red Dwarf official website','https://reddwarf.co.uk/about/the-crew','official_series_reference','Lister returns alive after stasis; Rimmer is an active computer-powered human-derived hologram; Holly operates the ship and Queeg is a prank identity.','Only early aboard-Red-Dwarf scope is used; Cat is feline-derived and machines are not counted as humans. Holly’s later failures and other ships are separate periods.'),
 ('exocolony','Job Events','I Was a Teenage Exocolonist Wiki contributors','https://exocolonist.fandom.com/wiki/Job_Events','secondary_game_reference','Colonists and children do actual farming, compost work, repair, care, construction and research alongside technology.','A player’s job menu and robot repair do not establish automated ordinary production or a labor-independent material floor.'),
 ('stardweller','Sufficiently Advanced first edition, indexed Stardweller/economy and technology passages','Colin Fredericks; rulebook text indexed at Scribd','https://www.scribd.com/doc/217741950/Sufficiently-Advanced-Ultra-Tech-RPG','indexed_rulebook_excerpt','Stardwellers ensure basics for all; replicators/transmutation underpin production, and peer esteem differs from material poverty.','Only bounded indexed first-edition passages used. The second-edition alternate setting uses Stardwellers more broadly and is not imported.'),
 ('stardweller-quote','FATAL & Friends — first-edition economy quotation','Something Awful participants quoting Sufficiently Advanced','https://forums.somethingawful.com/showthread.php?noseen=0&pagenumber=878&perpage=40&threadid=3758962','community_quote_of_rulebook','Quotation explicitly separates secure Stardweller basics from social/emotional exclusion and esteem.','Forum interpretation and political argument are not adopted as canon; no exact all-age labor statistic.'),
 ('tao','Sufficiently Advanced — review','RPGnet reviewer','https://www.rpg.net/reviews/archive/13/13780.phtml','secondary_review','The Tao’s citizens inhabit historical recreations and provide entertainment rather than merely visiting as avatars.','A sample reviewer character is not canon, and maintained historical style is not a verified common material entitlement.'),
 ('tao-primary','Sufficiently Advanced first edition, indexed Tao section','Colin Fredericks; rulebook text indexed at Scribd','https://www.scribd.com/doc/217741950/Sufficiently-Advanced-Ultra-Tech-RPG','indexed_rulebook_excerpt','Recreated societies have real generations, mostly nonsentient robot slaves where appropriate, variable local rule and controlled staged conflict.','Not all historical occupations are fake or robotic; the bounded section does not establish one ordinary household access rule across all milieus.')
]
